---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import FeedbackEngine from "../../novascope/components/FeedbackEngine.astro";

/*
 * HELD BACK 2026-09-04 pending Anna's review. This is NOT the old quarantine:
 * that was a defect hold and the defect is fixed.
 *
 * ── WHAT WAS WRONG, AND WHAT IT ACTUALLY WAS ──
 *
 * From 2026-08-21 this page was quarantined because the gas rendered nearly
 * invisible and the stars drew as hard-edged squares. The header said the cause
 * was "NOT YET DIAGNOSED", and that was right not to guess: `check:cluster-points`
 * passed bit-identical to its pin and `check:cluster-live` passed, because the
 * star layer was fine. It was drawing exactly what it was handed.
 *
 * The defect was in the DATA. `progenax`'s FK12 multi-freefall placement
 * normalizes globally and carries no term for how much gas a cell holds, so it
 * allocated the peak voxel 4043 Msun of stars against ~800 Msun of gas — a local
 * star-formation efficiency above 5, with 93.8% of the cluster inside one
 * 0.047 pc cell and a half-mass radius of 0.041 pc. Every star landed on a
 * handful of screen pixels; premultiplied additive blending then saturated the
 * whole sprite quad, so the visible edge stopped being the falloff and became
 * the quad. Squares. The same collapse is why the gas read as empty.
 *
 * Fixed upstream (progenax 92d5cd0, d66e106) and re-exported here (6188d47,
 * 4ff0719): 123 -> 2689 occupied cells, peak cell 93.8% -> 14.9%, r_half
 * 0.041 -> 0.755 pc. Verified by rendering this page in system Chrome at
 * 1512x857 on 2026-09-04: soft round stars across a resolved cluster, gas
 * visible, no squares.
 *
 * ── WHY IT IS STILL HELD BACK ──
 *
 * Because Anna has not signed it off yet, and that is the only reason. The four
 * mechanisms below stay in place TOGETHER, which is the part that matters:
 * `check-explore` requires a page with no hub card to also carry `noindex`, sit
 * in the sitemap filter and sit in the search crawler's EXCLUDED map. Dropping
 * those while leaving it uncarded would make it unreachable by navigation and
 * still advertised to search engines — a reader arriving from a search result
 * with no way to have got here deliberately, which is exactly the worst-of-both
 * state the old header recorded.
 *
 *   1. `noindex` below,
 *   2. the sitemap filter in astro.config.mjs,
 *   3. EXCLUDED in scripts/search/build-index.mjs — which fails the build if
 *      this page ever stops existing, so the hold cannot rot unnoticed,
 *   4. HELD_BACK in scripts/check-explore.mjs, which is what stops the build
 *      failing for the missing hub card.
 *
 * Remove all four together, and add the hub card in the same change, when Anna
 * says it is ready.
 *
 * ── STILL OPEN, and worth settling before sign-off ──
 *
 * - The copy at "Five of these six environments clear their gas" was measured
 *   against the OLD export and is not gated by check-feedback, which tests
 *   core-internal consistency only. Re-derive it.
 * - The page autoplays: FeedbackEngine sets `playing = true` with a 6 s loop, so
 *   a reader can land on a frame with the gas already gone.
 * - `canvas.dataset.engine` reads three.js's own version stamp, not ours;
 *   cloudScene sets it synchronously and three's async WebGPU init overwrites it.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Feedback budget — Anna Rosen"
  description="Do massive-star winds, photoionization, and radiation pressure deliver enough momentum to expel a cluster's natal gas — and does the cluster survive if they do? An interactive budget over six simulated environments."
  path="/explore/feedback-budget"
  noindex
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Explore · interactive"
    title="The feedback budget"
    lede="Massive stars pour winds, ionizing photons, and radiation pressure into the gas they were born in. This is the accounting: how much momentum each channel delivers, whether it clears the residual gas, and whether the young cluster stays bound once the gas is gone. The cluster and gas are a real gravoturb simulation; the budget is a per-channel momentum ledger, not a hydrodynamics run."
  />

  <div class="fbwrap wrap-wide">
    <FeedbackEngine />

    <div class="fbwrap__note prose wrap">
      <p>
        Two questions, in the order the physics asks them. <strong>First:</strong> is the
        retained feedback momentum enough to lift the residual gas out of the cloud's
        potential? <strong>Then, if it is:</strong> do the stars stay bound once the gas —
        most of the mass — is gone? Five of these six environments clear their gas. The
        most massive and most tightly bound cloud does not: the cost of lifting the gas
        grows with cloud mass faster than the cluster's output grows with its stars, and
        that cloud's escape speed also exceeds the sound speed of photoionized gas, so its
        H II region is trapped and one channel switches off entirely. Every cluster stays
        bound either way — and that is not a verdict the budget decides. These clusters
        are born far below virial equilibrium, so the stars' own self-gravity holds them
        whatever the gas does.
      </p>
      <p>
        The three channels deposit momentum on different schedules. Winds and radiation
        inject at a constant rate while the stars sit on the main sequence, so they
        accumulate linearly; the H II curve is the Spitzer solution for an
        ionization front recomputed at each step, and although the front decelerates, the
        shell it sweeps grows faster still, so photoionization accumulates faster than
        linearly until it runs out of cloud. Winds enter differently again: Weaver's
        similarity solution sets the ceiling on what a hot bubble could deliver, and how
        much survives cooling is calibrated so the momentum boost matches what
        three-dimensional simulations of wind-blown bubbles measure, rather than chosen
        to look reasonable.
      </p>
      <p>
        The controls above expose the two quantities this accounting is least sure of.
        <strong>Shell covering</strong> is one slider rather than two because it is one
        physical structure: the same holes that let starlight escape without ever
        touching the gas also let the hot shocked wind vent out of them. How strongly
        those are tied together is itself unsettled — the two published treatments offered
        here disagree about the wind boost by roughly a factor of five, and switching
        between them is the honest way to see that rather than hide it inside a default.
        <strong>H II leakage</strong> has no measurement behind it at all; it is a placed
        number, which is exactly why it should be something you can move. Both wind
        recipes are calibrated independently, so switching recipe switches its leakage
        with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gas balloons outward as the budget clears it, revealing the bare cluster — a
        legible stand-in for the anisotropic champagne flows and blister breakouts a full
        radiation-hydrodynamics run resolves. That physics is what my research code
        models; this piece makes its accounting visible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .fbwrap { padding-block: var(--space-l) var(--space-3xl); }
  .fbwrap__note { margin-block-start: var(--space-2xl); }
</style>
